WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.320
If you've ever been posting YouTube

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.800
videos and you're either not getting any

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:07.600
traction or you did get some traction,

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.280
then all of the sudden your views

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.480
absolutely dropped, then there is a

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:14.160
chance that you might have accidentally

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:15.600
turned on some hidden YouTube settings

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.080
that are killing your chances of

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.200
success. And I know this because last

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.480
year I actually started my own brother

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.960
out on YouTube, and with my help his

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.440
first ever video blew up. He immediately

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.760
had a ton of success on YouTube, but

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:31.760
then he turned on one setting, which

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.000
I'll talk about later in the video, that

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.720
absolutely killed his reach for a short

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.000
period of time until we figured it out

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.320
and then we were able to fix it. So, I'm

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.320
going to be talking about that setting

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.960
and also some other hidden settings that

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.000
people accidentally turn on that can

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.200
kill their reach. Now, some of these

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.320
things YouTube actually admits to, and

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.920
for others YouTube experts might say

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:51.760
that it doesn't make any difference at

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.640
all. But my anecdotal experience working

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.680
with literally thousands of YouTube

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.760
students now tells me otherwise. So,

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.480
we're going to go through this video,

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.960
make sure you aren't turning on these

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.680
settings. I'm also going to mention some

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.920
other ones that may not be killing your

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.440
reach, but they might be hurting it a

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:06.200
little bit. So, if you appreciate me

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.880
doing this type of video, let me know by

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:10.120
gently tapping that like button, and

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:12.600
let's jump into it right now with number

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.640
one, which I like to call the audience

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.480
splitter. Okay, so YouTube has this

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.720
relatively new feature called

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:21.080
auto-dubbing. Sounds really awesome.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.200
It'll basically dub your video in many

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:25.480
different languages: French, German,

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.840
Hindi, Indonesian, Italian, Japanese,

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:28.920
Portuguese, and Spanish. Great, right?

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.360
Instead of just having an

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.080
English-speaking audience, you can have

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:35.360
an audience all over the world. What

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:38.240
could possibly go wrong? Sounds good,

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:40.520
right? No, not quite, unfortunately. You

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.200
see, this feature can actually destroy

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:44.000
your channel. Now, let me explain what's

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.480
actually happening. Now, don't get me

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.160
wrong, for certain audiences this might

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.960
work great. For instance, if you teach

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:53.440
English to people who are not fluent in

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:55.160
English, like they barely speak any or

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.560
they don't speak any, this might work

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:58.760
great for you. But for a lot of

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.160
audiences, it's likely going to be

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:03.560
killing your video reach. Now, by the

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:05.440
way, maybe a year from now YouTube fixes

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:07.320
this, but right now this is what I'm

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:08.399
hearing from students, and this is what

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.720
I'm hearing from the community in

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:11.160
general. So, just as an example, if you

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:13.080
check out different YouTube subreddits,

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:15.200
you will see thread after thread after

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.920
thread of people basically saying that

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.040
auto-dubbing is killing their videos.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.120
Right? So, this creator said that

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.760
YouTube turned on auto-dubbing in April.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.600
They said that every single video since

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:26.200
April completely tanked. They couldn't

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.040
figure out why for months, then they

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:30.120
checked their analytics and it showed

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:32.400
that US viewers watched an average of 4

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.480
minutes and 33 seconds, and Brazilian

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:37.160
viewers watched for only 1 minute and 10

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:39.120
seconds. So, the Brazilian traffic had

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:40.840
terrible retention, right? It dragged

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.480
down the entire channel's performance.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:44.720
The algorithm thought their content was

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.280
bad, but it wasn't. The audience was

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.120
just wrong. And here's another problem

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:49.720
with this. Advertisers pay different

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.640
rates for different countries. US

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:53.080
viewers pay way more than viewers from

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:55.360
India, for instance. So, if your

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:57.040
audience shifts from US to India, your

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.480
revenue is going to drop. So, same

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:00.840
views, same watch time, but less money.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.720
And another creator shared their story,

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.840
too. Their video normally started with

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:06.240
10 to 15% click-through rate, and that's

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.920
really good for YouTube. But suddenly

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.200
their click-through rate dropped to less

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.320
than 3%. They checked their analytics,

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:14.480
almost every video was now in Hindi,

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:16.560
Indonesian, Japanese, and French. But

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.040
their videos were all in English. Wrong

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.360
audience, wrong language, wrong

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.840
everything. And of course, another side

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.640
effect of this is it becomes a very

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.920
difficult to see if your video's good or

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.720
not. You can't really look at the

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.600
analytics, they're kind of useless. So,

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:29.240
this client turned off auto-dubbing

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.640
immediately and their performance

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:32.560
started recovering almost

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:34.400
instantaneously, right? So, this feature

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.000
might sound cool, it might even work for

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.800
certain types of channels, but for most

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.400
